    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Sore throat, runny nose and cough for two days, a week after chemotherapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Radner called the clinic on day 8 of her second cycle of carboplatin and paclitaxel and was seen the same day. For two days she has had a scratchy sore throat, a runny nose and a dry cough, after a friend she works with had a cold. She checked her temperature twice a day as she was taught: the highest was 37.6 °C, never 38.0 °C. She has no shortness of breath, chest pain, burning on passing urine, diarrhea, or redness at the incision. She ate and drank normally yesterday; the nausea after cycle 2 lasted two days and settled with ondansetron. The surgical wound is healed. Her mood is steady, and she has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, after contact with a friend who had a cold</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Sore throat, runny nose, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Highest 37.6 °C at home; 37.5 °C today; never 38.0 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Day 8 of cycle 2 of carboplatin and paclitaxel, when the white count is expected to be near its lowest</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Warm drinks and throat lozenges help</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No breathlessness, chest pain, urinary symptoms, diarrhea, wound redness, or thoughts of self-harm</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

